{:edb/db-before-t 2666 :edb/db-after-t 2667 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535979 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:11:32.752Z" 13194139535979 true] [17592186220108 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220112 13194139535979 true] [17592186220113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220115 13194139535979 true] [17592186220118 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220119 13194139535979 true] [17592186220122 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220124 13194139535979 true] [17592186221096 1100 "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$, find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221096 1100 "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221096 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-5t} \\cdot t\\sin (2t)} &= (2(2)(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 2^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535979 false] [17592186221096 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221100 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0$. Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}$. Show the steps to compute this transform." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221100 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0.$ Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}.$ Show the steps to compute this transform." 13194139535979 true] [17592186221100 1145 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-7t}f(t)} &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-7t}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{- (s + 7)t}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $s > s_0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7)\n$$\n\nwhere\n$s + 7 > s_0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad s > s_0 - 7$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221100 1145 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-7t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221103 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?" 13194139535979 false] [17592186221103 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221103 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n- If $a < 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$, we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221103 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a < 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." 13194139535979 true] [17592186221108 1100 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221108 1100 "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221108 1145 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{2t^{2} + 5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= L{2t^{2}} + L{5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{n}} &= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}} \\\\\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 5 \\cdot (1)/((s - 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{s^{3}} + (5)/((s - 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221108 1145 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{2{t}^{2}}+L{5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377968 13194139535979 true] [17592186221373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377975 13194139535979 true] [17592186221373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377982 13194139535979 true] [17592186221373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377989 13194139535979 true] [17592186221374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378017 13194139535979 true] [17592186221374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378024 13194139535979 true] [17592186221374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378031 13194139535979 true] [17592186221374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378038 13194139535979 true] [17592186221375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377996 13194139535979 true] [17592186221375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378003 13194139535979 true] [17592186221375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378010 13194139535979 true] [17592186221375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378045 13194139535979 true] [17592186221376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377892 13194139535979 true] [17592186221376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377911 13194139535979 true] [17592186221376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377930 13194139535979 true] [17592186221376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377949 13194139535979 true] [17592186221863 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535979 true] [17592186221863 1100 "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$, find the Laplace transform $L{e^{4t} \\cdot t^{3}}$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221863 1100 "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}}.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221863 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{3!}{{s}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3!}{(s-4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535979 true] [17592186221866 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $L{e^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?" 13194139535979 false] [17592186221866 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221866 1145 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." 13194139535979 true] [17592186221867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535979 true] [17592186221867 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Suppose we wish to find $L{e^{2t}f(t)}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$e^{-st} \\cdot e^{2t}f(t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}" 13194139535979 false] [17592186221867 1100 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{2t}f(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221867 1145 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535979 true] [17592186221984 1100 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186221984 1100 "Given that\n\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t} + 2te^{2t}$ for $s > 2$." 13194139535979 false] [17592186221984 1145 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377893 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377898 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377903 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377907 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1089 "q-331788" 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535979 true] [17592186377892 1100 "Consider a functi